; The project verdict aliases, once, at the top of the test tree. Each
; folds two commands — the instrumented run of every suite under it,
; then the `windtrap` merge of what the suites wrote — into one:
;
;   dune build @cover --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.coverage
;   WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1 dune build @mutate --force --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate
;
; Coverage accumulates as a side effect of any instrumented run. A
; mutation VERDICT only exists if a suite was asked to test its mutants,
; so the mutation command carries WINDTRAP_MUTATE — the --mutate flag's
; mirror, which is what reaches every stanza under a build — the backend
; flag because the suites must carry the mutants, and --force because a
; mutation run is not a cached artifact; a plain `dune build @mutate`
; without them rebuilds the executables uninstrumented, which stales
; every verdict, and the merge then refuses loudly. The merge exits 1
; when a mutant survived every executable that reached it. (universe) is
; load-bearing in both: the .coverage and .mutants files are written by
; test executables at exit and are not declarable dependencies, so it
; makes the milliseconds-cheap merge re-run on every build.
;
; Inside windtrap's own tree the run half reaches the framework too —
; lib/ carries both backends — so the README's mutation command scopes
; the survey with WINDTRAP_MUTATE=examples/x-blueprint/lib; copied out,
; WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1 is the whole spelling.

(rule
 (alias cover)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (run %{bin:windtrap} coverage --min 80)))

(rule
 (alias mutate)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (run %{bin:windtrap} mutants)))
